The \(dE/dx\) distributions are not exactly gaussian. The \(log_{10}((dE/dx)/(dE/dx_{Bichsel}))\) (abbreviated as \(dE/dx\)), which follows gaussian distribution, was defined as the logarithmic ratio of measured \(dE/dx\) and predicted \(dE/dx\) from Bichsel model [1]. After the velocity cut, the 2-dimension histogram of \(dE/dx\) as a function of \(p_{T}\) was projected into several \(p_{T}\) bins. The 2-Gaussian function cannot describe the shoulder region between hadron peak and electron peak well at lower \(p_{T}\), a function of exponential+gaussian was performed instead. At \(p_{T}{{}^{>}_{\sim}}1.5\) GeV/\(c\), the other hadrons, such as protons, contaminate

